  ".github/copilot-instructions.md": "# snap-proj — GitHub Copilot instructions

<!-- nonoise:begin v0.0.0-snapshot -->
Project-scoped rules for Copilot. For cross-tool context see \`AGENTS.md\`.

## Entry point — Polly

This project ships with **Polly**, the NONoise SDLC advisor. When the developer is unsure
which skill to use, or asks "start polly" / "avvia polly" / "orchestrate this project",
read \`.claude/skills/polly/SKILL.md\` and follow its decision tree.

## Working with skills

This project ships a curated set of skills under \`.claude/skills/\`. When the user describes a task, your default path is to **engage a skill directly** based on the intent — do not route through Polly unless the user is explicitly lost ("where do I start?", "what's next?", "sono perso"). Examples: "I need to reverse engineer this codebase" → engage \`reverse-engineering\`; "draft requirements for X" → engage \`bmad-agent-analyst\`; "sprint breakdown for the ADR" → engage \`sprint-manifest\`.

**When you engage any SDLC skill, your first action is to ask the user for the full scope in one go — who, what, why, constraints, success criteria.** Use the skill's internal structure as a schema to fill, not as a turn-by-turn questionnaire. Only ask follow-up questions for genuine blockers (missing critical information you cannot infer). This applies to every SDLC skill — native, bundled, or vendored (superpowers/impeccable/skill-creator).

If the user is unsure which skill to use, they can invoke \`/polly\` (Claude Code), say "avvia polly" / "start polly" (Copilot and others), or you can offer Polly proactively as a fallback — never as the default.

## Conventions

- Follow the existing code style in the file you're editing.
- Write tests alongside implementations.
- Use clear, descriptive names.

## Available skills (cross-tool)

This project contains a library of AI skills in \`.claude/skills/\`. The skills are plain
markdown and apply to every AI tool, including Copilot. When the user's task matches the
description of one of these skills, open the corresponding \`SKILL.md\` file and follow its
instructions.

Installed skills:

**Orchestrator** — \`polly\`

**Requirements & discovery** — \`requirements-ingest\`, \`bmad-agent-analyst\`,
\`bmad-advanced-elicitation\`

**Architecture & validation** — \`arch-brainstorm\`, \`arch-decision\`, \`quint-fpf\`,
\`bmad-agent-architect\`

**Sprint & implementation** — \`sprint-manifest\`, \`atr\`

**Brownfield** — \`reverse-engineering\`

**Ops & observability** — \`ops-skill-builder\`, \`observability-debug\`

**Integrations** — \`spec-to-workitem\`, \`playwright-cli\`, \`frontend-design\`,
\`bmad-agent-ux-designer\`, \`bmad-agent-tech-writer\`

**Generators** — \`vscode-config-generator\`, \`docs-md-generator\`, \`design-md-generator\`

**Utility** — \`skill-finder\`

**Superpowers (native plugin from \`obra/superpowers\`)** — read these when the
workflow calls for them (Polly engages them during the SDLC loop):
\`superpowers:brainstorming\`, \`superpowers:writing-plans\`,
\`superpowers:executing-plans\`, \`superpowers:test-driven-development\`,
\`superpowers:verification-before-completion\`, \`superpowers:systematic-debugging\`,
\`superpowers:subagent-driven-development\`, \`superpowers:dispatching-parallel-agents\`,
\`superpowers:using-git-worktrees\`, \`superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch\`,
\`superpowers:requesting-code-review\`, \`superpowers:receiving-code-review\`,
\`superpowers:writing-skills\`, \`superpowers:using-superpowers\`

NONoise skills live at \`.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md\`. Vendor skills use native
plugins/installers by default; invoke \`superpowers:<name>\` through the harness.
With \`--vendor-mode embed\`, vendor copies live under \`.claude/skills/\`.

